# Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites every checked-out text file to
# CRLF. This repo compares two goldens as whole text — `tests/goldens/react_v2_turn_system.txt` and
# its user half, read with `include_str!` and `assert_eq!`d against a rendered prompt — so a Windows
# checkout would fail them for a reason that has nothing to do with the port. For a project whose
# claim is byte fidelity, the line ending is not incidental formatting; it is the thing under test.
#
# The JSON goldens survive either way, since `serde_json` treats CRLF as whitespace. The `.txt`
# pair does not, and nothing in the tree is binary — every tracked file is text, and there are no
# `.bat`/`.cmd`/`.ps1` scripts that would want CRLF — so one normalising rule covers the repo.
* text=auto eol=lf
